Unicode window titles: Xft font fallback, and a UTF-8 character type.

A window titled in Greek or Cyrillic -- luakit on a Greek page, the media
player playing a file with a Greek name -- showed only its spaces, digits and
punctuation in the title bar, the window list and the icon manager.

The title itself arrived intact: ctwm reads _NET_WM_NAME (UTF8_STRING) first,
converts it with XmbTextPropertyToTextList in the UTF-8 locale the session runs
in, and hands the UTF-8 string to Xft (patch 0003).  What dropped the
characters is the font.  An XftFont is ONE face, and Xft draws a character the
face has no glyph for with that face's .notdef.  The configured font, Selawik,
covers Latin only, and its .notdef is blank -- so every Greek and Cyrillic
letter was drawn as an empty advance.  Inside the application the same title
was complete, because Pango takes each character from the first font in
fontconfig's list that has it.

1. Font fallback for "xft:" fonts (util.c).  Text is laid out a character at a
   time: from the configured font when it has the glyph, otherwise from the
   first font in fontconfig's sorted list for the same pattern (FcFontSort,
   prepared as XftFontMatch prepares its match) whose coverage includes it.
   Fallback fonts are opened on first use and kept with the MyFont until it
   is reloaded.  The result is drawn with XftDrawCharFontSpec, and measured
   the same way, so widths, ellipsis and centring agree with what is drawn.
   The MyFont's metrics stay those of the configured font.  A byte that does
   not start a valid UTF-8 sequence is skipped instead of ending the string
   (Xft stops there), so a name cut inside a character loses one character.
   win_fluent.c measures and draws its title through the same layout.

2. The fontset redirections are object-like macros, and XmbDrawImageString is
   redirected too (ctwm.h).  ctwm names XmbDrawString without calling it, in
   "(cond ? XmbDrawImageString : XmbDrawString)(...)" (icon manager, classic
   title bars); a function-like macro does not expand there, so that text
   went to the core "fixed" fontset with the Xft font's metrics.
   XmbDrawImageString -- the size and position window, some menu modes -- was
   not redirected at all.  ctwm_XmbDrawImageString fills the logical
   rectangle with the GC's background and draws through Xft.

3. A UTF-8 character type whatever LANG says (ctwm_main.c, __LIKEOS__).  The
   C library's one encoding is UTF-8 whatever the locale is called, but Xlib
   chooses its converters by the locale NAME, and under "C" or "POSIX" (LANG
   unset) it converts titles to ISO 8859-1: every character outside it is
   dropped, and the Latin-1 bytes it keeps are not UTF-8 for Xft.  When
   LC_CTYPE does not name UTF-8, or Xlib does not support it, ctwm sets
   LC_CTYPE to "C.UTF-8" (Xlib's locale.alias maps it to en_US.UTF-8).

fontconfig is now linked directly (CMakeLists.txt).

