+/*
+ * Font fallback: the fonts that draw the characters an Xft font has no glyph
+ * for.
+ *
+ * An XftFont is a single face, and Xft draws a character that face lacks
+ * with the face's .notdef glyph.  Selawik, the desktop's interface font,
+ * covers Latin only and its .notdef is blank, so a window titled in Greek or
+ * Cyrillic showed its spaces and punctuation and nothing else -- while the
+ * same title inside the application, set by Pango, was complete, because a
+ * toolkit takes each character from the first font in fontconfig's list for
+ * the request that has it.  ctwm now does the same: fontconfig sorts every
+ * font against the configured pattern (FcFontSort, so the families named in
+ * it come first and the configuration's generic fallbacks after them), and a
+ * character the primary font lacks is drawn from the first font in that
+ * order whose coverage has it.  Fonts are opened when a character first
+ * needs them, and kept with the MyFont they serve until it is reloaded.
+ *
+ * One entry per registered MyFont, at the same index as in xft_fonts[].
